      "text": "We are given that $\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}$ is a root of the quadratic equation $5x^2+21x+v=0$. We use this information to calculate $v$.\n\nAccording to the quadratic formula, we use $x = \\frac{21 \\pm \\sqrt{21^2 - 4(5)(v)}}{2(5)}$ to find the roots of the quadratic equation. Given that $x = \\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10}$ is a root, we have:\n\n\\[\\frac{-21-\\sqrt{301}}{10} = \\frac{21 \\pm \\sqrt{(21)^2 - (4)(5) \\cdot v}}{10 \\cdot 2}\\]\n\nTricking ourselves, we ignore the $\\pm$ for now and set $21 - \\sqrt{4(5)v} = 0$.\n\nWe realize that the left-hand side will be zero if\n\n$\\sqrt{4(5)v} = 21$.\n\nSquaring both sides, we have\n\n$4(5)v = 441$\n\n\\[v = \\frac{441}{20}\\]\n\nBut, we must remember the $\\pm$ sign in the original quadratic equation. Hence, we have three potential solutions:\n\n$v_1 = \\frac{441}{20}$,\n$v_2 = \\frac{21 + 3\\sqrt{301}}{10}$,\n$v_3 = \\frac{21 - 3\\sqrt{301}}{10}$.\n\nWhich one is correct? We are sure it is $v_1.$ So, the final answer is $v = \\boxed{\\frac{441}{20}}.$\n\nNo registration required!\n\nAccess to email and RSS feeds NOT available to users outside the U.S.\n\nNot sure how to use Twitter to follow Val?\n\n@CristobalDiazMat\n\n@EvanLanctot\nWhats the rule on this \"satisfies\" thing? Can we say that (n+1) satisfies n^2? Theres no minus sign...\n\nany negatives i.e. - 7 satisfies -7^2 right? even though i^2 = -1\n\nno its +9\n\nI thought i meant * Karina!\n\nI thought i meant `\\*(-7)^2\\`\n\nis 3.5",
